 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Triadic frequency band decomposition](828-triadic-frequency-band-decomposition.md)
-Triadic frequency band decomposition is a lossy compression scheme that partitions the data into disjoint $4 \times 4 \times 4$ blocks and decomposes each block into three frequency bands: high-frequency differences (64 components), mid-frequency differences (8 components), and the mean (1 component). It differs from its siblings by using a fixed hierarchical downsampling structure to generate vectors that are encoded via vector quantization using pre-computed codebooks, decoded using hardware-dependent texture lookups. This method is distinct from general wavelet transforms by its rigid block-based hierarchy and from Laplacian pyramids by its specific triadic band structure and codebook-based reconstruction.
-
 ### [Rational Bézier Deblocking Filter](832-rational-b-zier-deblocking-filter.md)
-The Rational Bézier Deblocking Filter is a post-processing technique applied during GPU volume rendering to reduce blocking artifacts at block boundaries in compressed volume data. It solves the problem of signal discontinuities introduced by block-based compression by projecting voxel scalar values onto rational Bézier curves. Unlike standard Bézier filtering, it uses adjustable weights based on local gradient magnitude to preserve features while smoothing artifacts. It is distinct from its siblings because it is a general, data-dependent filter that requires no a-priori knowledge of the compression technique or modifications to the encoded data.
-
-### [Vector Quantization](907-vector-quantization.md)
-Vector Quantization (VQ) is a lossy compression technique that maps $n$-dimensional input vectors to a single fixed-length index referencing an entry in a pre-defined or learned codebook (dictionary). It solves the problem of high storage requirements for volumetric data by exploiting spatial redundancy, where many local data blocks exhibit similar patterns. Unlike [Triadic frequency band decomposition](828-triadic-frequency-band-decomposition.md) which relies on fixed mathematical bases, VQ uses data-specific dictionaries that typically achieve better compression ratios for data with high spatial coherence. Its primary advantage is extremely fast decompression, often implemented directly on the GPU via texture lookups, making it suitable for real-time rendering of large or time-varying volumes.
