 ### Block Truncation Coding
-Block Truncation Coding (BTC) is an adaptation of BTC supporting hardware-accelerated random access, specifically texture compression using invalid combinations [S4]. In BTC, data is decomposed into equal-sized blocks, and each block is truncated as long as a given criteria (e.g., standard deviation and average value) is satisfied [S1, S4]. This results in a different error tolerance (threshold level) per block [S4]. Adaptations include VTC (3D extension of S3TC), ETC2, and ASTC [S1, S4].
+An adaptation of BTC supporting hardware accelerated random access, specifically texture compression using invalid combinations [S4].
 
 ### Volumetric Coding Scheme
-The Volumetric Coding Scheme separates volumetric data into a model $m$ (binary volume: 1=existing, 0=transparent) and texture $T$ (luminance Y, chrominance U,V) to allow lossless model coding and scalable bitstreams [S5]. Luminance Y is used to identify the model structure; it is input to depth-plane reduction, while chrominance U,V are subsampled before depth-plane reduction [S5]. The model $m$ is losslessly and independently coded, while texture $T$ is lossily coded [S5]. The data stream is organized as: 1) Half resolution volume data ($m_h, T_h$), 2) Additional data for full resolution ($m_f, T_f$), 3) Remaining data for double resolution ($m_d, T_d$) [S5]. Camera parameters are separately coded using Huffman coding as side information [S5]. The background is independently coded from the volumetric data [S5].
