 ### 4×4×4 Block Reconstruction
-4×4×4 Block Reconstruction is a block-wise decoding optimization for wavelet-compressed volumes that processes voxels in 4×4×4 blocks to reuse R-slice values across adjacent blocks, reducing decoding overhead for spatially coherent access patterns [S7]. The volume is partitioned into 4×4×4 blocks, each containing 3 I-slices followed by 1 R-slice [S7]. Decoding begins by computing the 4×4 voxels of the R-slice, then computes the 4×4 voxels for each of the 3 I-slices in the current block by reusing the retained R-slice values from the block above [S7]. This optimization assumes regular or spatially coherent access; purely random access patterns negate the benefit [S7]. Random voxel access on compressed data is 3 to 5 times slower than uncompressed data on average, and whole-volume reconstruction takes roughly 30-40 seconds longer than original volumes [S7]. However, this slowdown is acceptable compared to disk/network latency [S7].
